# Month 3 — Human Judgment Exercise

## Scenario
You have passed Security+. A classmate tells you: 
"Security+ is useless in South Africa — employers 
only care about experience."

## My Response

Security+ removes the HR filter — without it, my CV 
doesn't reach a human reviewer at most enterprise 
employers. That's not a theoretical 
advantage, it's a practical gate. Clearing it was 
the correct first move.

What it doesn't prove: that I can triage a real 
incident at 2am, that I understand the political 
dynamics of escalating to a CISO, or that I can 
tune a detection rule without generating 400 false 
positives a day. No cert proves any of that.

What I'm doing in parallel: I've deployed a Wazuh 
4.14.7 SIEM lab with four custom detection rules 
mapped to MITRE ATT&CK, written a formal 
vulnerability assessment report based on real CIS 
benchmark scan results, and documented a real 
infrastructure incident (an accidental package 
upgrade that took down my lab for 3 hours). 

That work doesn't replace SOC experience. But it 
demonstrates that when I get into a SOC role, the 
first 90 days won't be spent learning what a SIEM 
is. That's the gap the lab work closes — not the 
experience gap, the credibility gap.
